WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.449 --> 00:00:09.800
V lands are virtual separations within a switch

2
00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.720
and are used mainly to limit broadcast traffic.

3
00:00:13.119 --> 00:00:15.880
A V land can be configured to communicate with other V

4
00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:19.639
lands or not and may be used to segregate network segments.

5
00:00:20.549 --> 00:00:24.120
There are a few common uses of V lands in corporate networks.

6
00:00:24.379 --> 00:00:30.329
The first is to separate voice over I PV O IP telephones from the corporate network.

7
00:00:30.739 --> 00:00:34.970
This is most often done to more effectively manage the network traffic generated

8
00:00:34.979 --> 00:00:38.729
by voice communications by isolating it from the rest of the network.

9
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:40.979
Another common use of V

10
00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.139
lands in a corporate network is to separate

11
00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:45.810
the data center from all other network traffic.

12
00:00:46.139 --> 00:00:49.869
This makes it easier to keep the server to server traffic contained to the data

13
00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:52.549
center network while allowing certain traffic from

14
00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.360
workstations or the web to access the servers

15
00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:57.900
as briefly discussed earlier. V

16
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.479
lands can also be used to segment networks.

17
00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:01.900
For example,

18
00:01:01.909 --> 00:01:04.660
A V land can separate the payroll workstations from

19
00:01:04.669 --> 00:01:06.660
the rest of the workstations in the network.

20
00:01:07.099 --> 00:01:11.699
Routing rules can also be used to only allow devices within this payroll va

21
00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:15.050
to access the servers containing payroll information.

22
00:01:16.089 --> 00:01:19.669
Earlier, we also discussed network access control

23
00:01:20.029 --> 00:01:20.319
nec

24
00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:24.209
these systems use V LS to control whether devices connect

25
00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:26.959
to the corporate network or to a guest network.

26
00:01:27.129 --> 00:01:30.230
Even though a wireless access controller may attach to

27
00:01:30.239 --> 00:01:32.919
a single port on a physical network switch.

28
00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:35.580
The VL associated with the device connection

29
00:01:35.589 --> 00:01:38.690
on the wireless access controller determines the va

30
00:01:38.769 --> 00:01:42.680
that the device operates on and to which networks it is allowed to connect.

31
00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:46.330
Finally, in large corporate networks,

32
00:01:46.339 --> 00:01:50.760
V lands can be used to limit the amount of broadcast traffic within a network.

33
00:01:51.069 --> 00:01:53.379
This is most common in networks of more than

34
00:01:53.389 --> 00:01:57.470
1000 devices and may be separated by department location,

35
00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:00.239
building or any other criteria as needed.

36
00:02:01.139 --> 00:02:05.480
The most important thing to remember is that while V lands are logically separated,

37
00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:08.258
they may be allowed to access other V lands,

38
00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:12.279
they can also be configured to deny access to other V lands.